cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Plarena in teaching or research, please cite it as below."
title: "Plarena: playable arenas for teaching hard ideas in computer science"
authors:
  - family-names: "Su"
    given-names: "Xian"
    affiliation: "Florida International University"
type: software
version: "0.1.3"
date-released: "2026-08-24"
url: "https://plarena.app/"
repository-code: "https://github.com/plarena-labs/plarena-labs.github.io"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - computing education
  - algorithms education
  - artificial intelligence education
  - machine learning education
  - search algorithms
  - "A*"
  - adversarial search
  - explorable explanation
  - visualization
abstract: >-
  Plarena is a suite of zero-install, single-file browser apps for teaching
  computer science through play: students do not watch an algorithm, they run
  it, break it, race it, and write their own agents against it. Each arena is
  one self-contained HTML file that works offline from disk and makes no
  network requests. Search Arena covers uninformed and informed search on
  procedurally generated grid worlds, with a probe-level search tree, a live
  frontier data-structure panel, and fog-of-war observability. Algo Arena runs
  nine rooms on a single certified campus map, tracing the arc from brute force
  through greedy, divide-and-conquer, dynamic programming and reduction to the
  intractability wall. Game Arena covers adversarial search on tic-tac-toe,
  where every leaf is a true terminal utility, so minimax and alpha-beta values
  are exact. ML Arena is a small recommender in which the student's own clicks
  are the training set, and linear regression, logistic regression and a small
  neural network each win where the others fail. Teaching numbers in Algo Arena and
  ML Arena are reproduced by an independent Python twin of the JavaScript core;
  Search and Game Arena are reproduced by the app's own deterministic engine.
  Deterministic seeds with URL share-links make each instance exactly
  reproducible, so a link can be handed out as an assignment. Source code is
  MIT; the course content and datasets are CC BY 4.0; the Plarena name and
  mascot are reserved (see BRAND.md).
